# Clairic field types: text, number, date, list, estimate and forecast

Clairic, Cloudflare's extraction model, answers typed fields instead of questions. There are nine field types: text, number, date, time, datetime, duration, list, estimate and forecast. Single values come back with p_null, the chance the value is missing, plus confidence and guess. Estimates and forecasts come back as distributions with a median and an 80% interval. Every fact here is from the Workers AI docs as of 20 October 2026.

## Fields, not questions

A decision model takes questions and picks from options you list. Choice, Score and Noul are its three shapes. An extraction model takes fields instead. Clairic, Cloudflare’s first, reads the input and fills in a typed value for each field.

A Clairic request has a `state`, the text to read, and `fields`, a map from field name to field spec. Every spec needs a `type`. Most also carry `instructions`, a plain sentence on what the field should contain. A field name starts with a letter or underscore and runs to 64 characters at most.

There is no cap on the number of fields. Fields that do not fit beside the state in one 8,192-token pass are split across parallel passes. The field specs bill as input tokens: in the docs’ invoice example they took 472 of 655.

Cloudflare’s concepts page groups the work into four jobs. Extraction returns a value printed in the input. Computation works one out from facts in the input. Estimation predicts a value the input does not settle. Forecasting predicts a value for each step of a future period. The nine types below cover all four.

## The nine types at a glance

Type  | What comes back  | Settings
--- | --- | ---
`text`  | A span of the input, with character offsets  | `instructions`
`number`  | A number, read or computed  | `unit`, `integer`, `decimals` (0 to 6)
`date`  | An ISO date  | `instructions`
`time`  | A time  | `instructions`
`datetime`  | An ISO date and time  | `instructions`
`duration`  | A length of time in your unit  | `unit`, `integer`, `decimals`
`list`  | Every match, in input order  | `items` (`text`, `number` or `date`), required
`estimate`  | A distribution inside your bounds  | `min` and `max` (required), `step`, `scale`
`forecast`  | One estimate per future step  | `min`, `max`, `horizon`, `interval`

Every type also takes `null_threshold`, covered below.

