The extraction model guide covers the class.

## What it returns

You send a `state` (text, plus up to four images) and `fields`, a map of named fields. Each field has a type and optional plain-language `instructions`.

Field type  | What comes back
--- | ---
`text`  | A span of the input, with its offsets
`number`  | A value read or computed, with integer, decimals and unit options
`date`, `time`, `datetime`  | The value in ISO form
`duration`  | A length of time in the unit you choose
`list`  | Matching text, numbers or dates in input order, with spans
`estimate`  | A distribution inside your minimum and maximum, on your step grid
`forecast`  | One estimate per future step of a series

Every scalar answer carries `value`, `p_null` (the probability the value is not in the input), `confidence` and `guess`. When the value is missing, `value` is null and `guess` shows what the model would otherwise have picked. Estimates add a `median`, an 80% interval from `p10` to `p90`, and `p_below` and `p_above`; all 99 percentiles are available on request. Forecasts return the same numbers as arrays, one per step.

The API is not Jev-compatible. It takes `fields`, not `questions`, and answers under `answers`.

